        "header": "1Introduction",
        "images": [
            {
                "img": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.33855/2609.33855v1/solver_training_final.png",
                "caption": "Figure 2:Majority-vote labels vs. computed answers.(a)In prior self-play, the solver’s majority answer becomes the label. Here three of five answers say 2 mugs, so the wrong answer is rewarded and the correct answer“1”is discarded.(b)VQS computes the answer instead.(1)A schema-constrained parser turns the image into a structured parse. A fixed template reads the parse (filter mugs, then count) to write a question and compute its answer“1”and the parser then paraphrases the question.(2)A visual checker verifies each claim behind the answer, a blind gate drops questions answerable without the image, and a difficulty band keeps those the solver gets right in some but not all of 8 rollouts.(3)GRPO rewards each rollout by exact match against the computed answer, so training reinforces the correct answer“1”and suppresses the wrong one“2”.",
                "position": 293
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "header": "2Related work",
        "images": [
            {
                "img": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.33855/2609.33855v1/vqs-parser.png",
                "caption": "Figure 3:Label-free parser training.(1)The parser samplesKKstructured parses of an unlabeled image under a schema-constrained decoder.(2)A visual checker verifies each parse one claim at a time. In the example,z2z_{2}wrongly calls the mug blue and places it to the right of the book, so it passes 6 of 8 claims.(3)Each parse is scored by its fraction of verified claims. We keep the best parsez1z_{1}(8/8) only because it beats the mean of all four parses (0.75) and contains at leastm=4m{=}4claims.(4)The kept parses train the parser with SFT. The trained parser then feeds the templates that write questions and compute their answers for the solver.",
                "position": 306
            }
